WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:12.360
Modern applications often mix traditional server sessions with token-based approaches. A server-rendered web application may use an opaque cookie-backed session. A single-page application may call APIs with access tokens.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:20.340
A mobile application may rely on tokens stored in platform-managed storage. Backend services may exchange credentials through an identity provider.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:33.200
The architecture changes the mechanics, but the security questions stay familiar: who is this request for, what is it allowed to do, and is the session state still valid? Access tokens and refresh tokens need different handling.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:44.780
Access tokens are commonly short-lived credentials used to call APIs or access resources. Refresh tokens can allow the client to obtain new access tokens over time, so they are especially sensitive.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:54.360
Their storage, rotation, revocation, and reuse detection policies matter. Developers should not assume that a token is safe just because it uses a common format.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:01:07.380
Issuer, audience, expiration, signature, scope, and relevant claims still need validation. Single-page applications, mobile applications, and APIs introduce storage and cross-origin considerations.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:16.960
Browser-accessible storage may be convenient, but it can create exposure if the application has client-side script weaknesses. Native mobile storage should use platform protections where available.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:26.700
APIs should validate tokens server-side and enforce authorization on every sensitive operation. Modern session management is not about choosing cookies or tokens as a slogan.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:34.520
It is about matching the storage, lifetime, validation, and renewal model to the application?s threat profile.
